          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Should I be on something else as well?",
          "answer": "I can't tell you whether you should be on another medicine. That depends on your symptoms and how well your asthma is controlled, and your doctor or asthma nurse is the right person to decide.\n\nThe asthma guidance in the \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\" document (from the Global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel guidance) says:\n\n- Treatment is chosen by looking at how well your symptoms are controlled and how often you have flare-ups. The plan is reviewed over time rather than fixed.\n- Lower steps of treatment can include a combination inhaler, or a regular low-dose inhaled corticosteroid taken alongside an as-needed reliever.\n- Before adding treatment, clinicians check your inhaler technique and whether you're taking your medicines as prescribed. The guidance notes these account for a large share of apparent treatment failure.\n\nThe documents I searched don't say whether a blue inhaler alone is enough for you. Questions to bring to your next appointment:\n- How well is my asthma controlled, and how do you judge that?\n- Do I need a regular controller medicine, or is my current plan right for me?\n- Can you check how I use my inhaler?\n- How often should my asthma plan be reviewed?\n\nIf you have trouble breathing, your blue inhaler isn't easing symptoms, or you're getting breathless more often, get urgent medical help now by calling your local emergency number.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
